Below are a series of exploratory → solution‐building queries (max 10, each limited to 20 rows) that progressively answer  
“How many distinct image URLs are ‘cat’ images (label = ‘/m/01yrx’, confidence = 1) and how many are ‘other’ (no such cat label at all)?”  

```sql
-- 1.  Look-up the label code for the word CAT in the dictionary
SELECT label_name, label_display_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.dict`
WHERE LOWER(label_display_name) = 'cat'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Confirms that the ‘Cat’ label code is ‘/m/01yrx’.
```

```sql
-- 2.  Inspect a few rows that carry the cat label in the labels table
SELECT image_id, source, label_name, confidence
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.labels`
WHERE label_name = '/m/01yrx'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: See how the cat label appears in the labels table.
```

```sql
-- 3.  Inspect a few rows that carry the cat label in the bbox table
SELECT image_id, source, label_name, confidence, x_min, y_min, x_max, y_max
FROM `bigquery-public-data.open_images.annotations_bbox`
WHERE label_name = '/m/01yrx'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: The bbox table contains confidence = 1.0 rows for cats – useful for high-confidence filtering.
```

```sql
-- 4.  Pull 20 sample image URLs that are labelled as a CAT with confidence 1
SELECT DISTINCT i.original_url
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images`            AS i
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.open_images.annotations_bbox`  AS b
ON     i.image_id = b.image_id
WHERE  b.label_name = '/m/01yrx'
  AND  b.confidence = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Shows example URLs of images that definitely contain a cat.
```

```sql
-- 5.  Pull 20 sample image URLs that have NO cat label at all
SELECT DISTINCT i.original_url
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images` AS i
LEFT JOIN `bigquery-public-data.open_images.annotations_bbox` AS b
       ON i.image_id = b.image_id
          AND b.label_name = '/m/01yrx'
          AND b.confidence = 1
WHERE  b.image_id IS NULL                    -- no cat record
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: Sample URLs that are “other” (they never got a cat label with confidence 1).
```

```sql
-- 6.  Count the number of DISTINCT image URLs that have a cat label
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT i.original_url) AS cat_url_cnt
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images`           AS i
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.open_images.annotations_bbox` AS b
ON     i.image_id = b.image_id
WHERE  b.label_name = '/m/01yrx'
  AND  b.confidence = 1;
```

```sql
--Description: Gives the total # of unique URLs classified as cat.
```

```sql
-- 7.  Count the total number of DISTINCT image URLs in the whole images table
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT original_url) AS total_url_cnt
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images`;
```

```sql
--Description: Needed to compute the “other” count (total – cat).
```

```sql
-- 8.  Final answer: cat vs. other URL counts in one query
WITH cat_urls AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT i.original_url
    FROM   `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images`           AS i
    JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.open_images.annotations_bbox` AS b
    ON     i.image_id = b.image_id
    WHERE  b.label_name   = '/m/01yrx'
      AND  b.confidence   = 1
),
all_urls AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT original_url
    FROM   `bigquery-public-data.open_images.images`
)
SELECT 
    (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM cat_urls)                    AS cat_url_cnt,
    (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM all_urls) -
    (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM cat_urls)                    AS other_url_cnt;
```

```sql
--Description: Returns two numbers – how many distinct URLs are “cat” and how many are “other”.
```

Execute the last query to obtain the desired counts.
